From the k158 review. writeFileAtomic (cmd/launcher/hermes.go ~:981, :989-992) keeps the permissions of an existing file and only creates a new one as 0600. So when unreagent.local.yaml already exists as 0644 (the usual umask), hermes-setup writes the generated mcp.token into a file other local users can read. Options: tighten an existing local file to 0600 when the token is written into it (and say so in the output), or warn naming the mode and the chmod. Unix only — on Windows the file inherits the directory ACL; say what holds there. Done: chosen behaviour + test (skip on Windows), README hermes-setup section and the unreagent-config skill's Secrets section updated. Lane: unreagent-worker (+ doc-writer, skill-author).

Main agent's decision (2026-11-21): when hermes-setup writes the token into an existing unreagent.local.yaml whose mode lets group or others read it, it tightens the file to 0600 and says so in its output (old mode -> 0600). Unix only. On Windows the mode bits say nothing about readability (the file inherits the directory ACL), so nothing changes there and the docs say so. Bundled with k165 (same file, one worker).

Worker (code done, uncommitted, docs pending): writeFileAtomic(path, data, secret bool) (os.FileMode, error). With secret set and an existing file whose mode&0o077 != 0, the temp file gets 0600 instead of the old bits and the old mode is returned (0 = nothing tightened). Only the unreagent.local.yaml token write passes true; the Hermes config write passes false. Why in writeFileAtomic and not at the call site: the mode is applied to the temp file before the rename, so the token is never on disk under the old bits and the tightening lands with the write or not at all. A call-site chmod after the write leaves a window; one before it changes the file even when the write then fails, and would repeat writeTarget's symlink resolution. Output line after 'New bearer token generated ...': 'unreagent.local.yaml was open to group or others (mode 0644); tightened it to 0600, since it now holds the bearer token.' A new file is still 0600, a closed one (0600, 0400) keeps its mode silently. Windows: runtime.GOOS guard (the style of hermes.go's neighbours), nothing tightened; compile-only, not run. Tests: TestHermesSetupTightensOpenLocalConfig (0644 and 0640 end 0600 with the line, 0600 stays silent; skips on Windows); TestHermesSetupKeepsFileMode now sets the local file to 0400 (kept) and the Hermes config to 0644 (kept), so it pins that only the token write tightens. Mutations (drop the secret test, drop the 0o077 test) each turn a test red. Check: gofmt clean, go vet + GOOS=windows go vet clean, go test ./... and -race ./cmd/launcher green, make linux + make windows + CGO_ENABLED=0 windows build OK. Drift for the main agent, not in scope: the Hermes config holds the same bearer and keeps a 0644 mode (by the decision only the local file tightens); a user-set mcp.token in a 0644 local file is never written, so never tightened. README + unreagent-config Secrets follow (doc-writer, skill-author); not handed to review yet.

Main agent's decision on the worker's two gaps: (1) the Hermes config also holds the bearer token and may be 0644, but it belongs to another program, so hermes-setup does not change its mode; whether to warn about it is card k169. (2) A token the user wrote into a 0644 unreagent.local.yaml by hand is not hermes-setup's write, so it stays out of scope; the docs say that only hermes-setup's own token write tightens.

